	doi = {10.4018/979-8-3373-0189-1.ch019},
	abstract = {This chapter presents lessons from the SDG Labs project, a transnational initiative fostering green skills by integrating social economy entities and higher education. Through socially driven green Labs, the project explored innovative educational methods, including Living Labs, simulation-based learning, and participatory co-creation. Findings highlight gaps between theoretical environmental knowledge and practical sustainability skills. While social economy entities grasp basic concepts like the circular economy, advanced green competencies-such as environmental impact assessment-are less integrated. Effective interventions involve hands-on learning through green business simulations and multi-stakeholder collaboration Institutional barriers, including limited policy support and regulatory challenges, often hinder scaling sustainable projects. Addressing these requires strengthening institutional frameworks and enhancing environmental literacy. The project underscores the need to align education with social economy demands, setting a benchmark for knowledge-driven green economies. © 2025, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
